WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:22.298
Hello. My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry. I am an architect. With ten years of architectural experience. Welcome, to this episode. Implementing microservices, with domain-driven design as the model.

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.392
A delivery drone drops a package.

3
00:00:36.432 --> 00:00:43.247
Here is a business rule: a customer can cancel a delivery only until the drone has picked the package up.

4
00:00:44.287 --> 00:00:46.098
Where does that rule live?

5
00:00:47.138 --> 00:00:51.538
In which service, in which object, checked by whom?

6
00:00:52.088 --> 00:01:01.306
Domain-driven design is the school that answers those questions — and this episode follows the rule all the way from the HTTP request to the message that leaves the service.

7
00:01:02.286 --> 00:01:08.498
Domain-driven design is a way to implement a microservice, and a way to decide where one service ends.

8
00:01:08.878 --> 00:01:12.128
Eric Evans set it out in 2003.

9
00:01:13.168 --> 00:01:21.888
Vaughn Vernon’s ‘Implementing Domain-Driven Design’, 2013, is the practical reference the microservice generation used.

10
00:01:22.588 --> 00:01:32.770
And Microsoft’s Azure Architecture Center wrote the size rule this research keeps: design a microservice to be no smaller than an aggregate, and no larger than a bounded context.

11
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:34.887
Two passes.

12
00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:38.528
Strategic design divides the business.

13
00:01:39.568 --> 00:01:51.050
The domain splits into subdomains: a core subdomain the business competes on, supporting subdomains it needs, and generic subdomains that are solved problems, like identity or notification, often bought.

14
00:01:51.750 --> 00:02:03.627
Each piece of the model that must stay internally consistent is a bounded context, with its own ubiquitous language — the words the code, the API, and the team share.

15
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:08.057
A bounded context is a candidate for a service.

16
00:02:09.037 --> 00:02:13.379
Tactical design builds the model inside that context.

17
00:02:14.419 --> 00:02:21.873
An entity has a stable identity over time — this Delivery, that Drone — and the business rules live on it.

18
00:02:22.253 --> 00:02:28.255
A value object has no identity — a location, a weight, an amount of money.

19
00:02:28.955 --> 00:02:39.753
An aggregate is the consistency boundary: one root entity, child objects reached only through the root, everything saved in one local transaction.

20
00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:49.187
A domain service holds logic that belongs to no single entity — the Scheduler that coordinates drone, window, and route.

21
00:02:49.567 --> 00:02:57.380
An application service is the use case: it opens the transaction and calls the domain, but does not contain the rule.

22
00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.416
A domain event names a meaningful change.

23
00:03:02.456 --> 00:03:06.008
A repository loads and saves an aggregate by identity.

24
00:03:06.988 --> 00:03:11.714
Vernon’s aggregate rules keep the service small enough.

25
00:03:12.754 --> 00:03:18.686
Include in the aggregate only what must be consistent in a single transaction.

26
00:03:19.726 --> 00:03:28.201
Keep aggregates small — Delivery, Package, Drone, and Account are separate, because each has its own life cycle.

27
00:03:28.901 --> 00:03:35.844
Refer to another aggregate by identity: Delivery stores a DroneId, not the Drone.

28
00:03:36.884 --> 00:03:42.527
And when a business process crosses aggregates, publish an event and accept that the other side will catch up.

29
00:03:42.907 --> 00:03:45.217
That seam is where a saga begins.

30
00:03:46.537 --> 00:03:48.104
Now the request.

31
00:03:49.144 --> 00:03:52.674
An inbound adapter — the HTTP endpoint — receives it.

32
00:03:53.054 --> 00:03:57.733
An application service starts the use case and a local database transaction.

33
00:03:58.433 --> 00:04:07.117
The domain enforces the rule: Delivery decides whether it can be cancelled; the application service does not reimplement that decision.

34
00:04:08.157 --> 00:04:12.116
The aggregate stores foreign identities only.

35
00:04:13.156 --> 00:04:20.180
A repository saves it, and a domain event is recorded in the same transaction, typically through an outbox.

36
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.749
The transaction commits.

37
00:04:23.789 --> 00:04:31.405
Then a publisher sends an integration event on the broker, and the accounts context can consume it later and raise an invoice.

38
00:04:31.785 --> 00:04:35.361
The two contexts are never in one transaction.

39
00:04:36.681 --> 00:04:44.622
Inside one service, an in-process domain event can notify another aggregate in the same model — Microsoft’s eShop does this with MediatR.

40
00:04:45.002 --> 00:04:52.038
The event that leaves the service is a separate integration event, carried by RabbitMQ or Azure Service Bus.

41
00:04:52.418 --> 00:04:59.036
Keeping those two events apart is the implementation of ‘smart endpoints and dumb pipes’ at the level of one codebase.

42
00:05:00.016 --> 00:05:04.126
DDD is the right interior when the service holds real business rules.

43
00:05:04.506 --> 00:05:06.723
Other interiors sit beside it.

44
00:05:07.763 --> 00:05:23.054
Hexagonal architecture — Alistair Cockburn, 2005 — is the packaging: business logic in the middle, inbound adapters and outbound adapters at the edge, and the domain never imports the web framework or the database driver.

45
00:05:24.094 --> 00:05:33.683
CQRS, simplified: commands through aggregates, queries as plain SQL, same database — eShop’s ordering service does exactly this.

46
00:05:34.063 --> 00:05:39.671
CQRS with a separate read model, when reads must not touch every owner.

47
00:05:40.711 --> 00:05:47.654
And the transaction script: check a few fields, write a row, no aggregate — the right choice when the rules are thin.

48
00:05:48.634 --> 00:05:54.880
Evans’s context map draws the relationships between contexts, and four of them become integration styles.

49
00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:00.101
Open host service with a published language: a stable API and event schema.

50
00:06:00.481 --> 00:06:05.009
Customer–supplier: the downstream needs a change and the upstream agrees.

51
00:06:05.709 --> 00:06:10.411
Conformist: the downstream accepts the upstream model as it stands.

52
00:06:10.791 --> 00:06:15.261
Anti-corruption layer: the downstream translates a foreign model into its own.

53
00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:20.459
A command that must cross services is a saga on top of these events.

54
00:06:21.779 --> 00:06:24.368
Here is the test the chapter hands forward.

55
00:06:25.408 --> 00:06:30.459
A tool that generates a controller and a database table has implemented the thin script.

56
00:06:30.839 --> 00:06:38.745
A tool that has implemented DDD has named the aggregates, kept foreign references as identities, and split the domain event from the integration event.

57
00:06:39.445 --> 00:06:44.971
Did AI change how boundaries are found, the interior of the service, or the seam between services?

58
00:06:45.521 --> 00:06:47.832
Three separate questions.

59
00:06:49.152 --> 00:06:51.044
Three things to carry away.

60
00:06:52.084 --> 00:06:52.699
One.

61
00:06:53.739 --> 00:06:58.569
A service is no smaller than an aggregate and no larger than a bounded context.

62
00:06:59.269 --> 00:06:59.826
Two.

63
00:07:00.866 --> 00:07:05.975
Refer to other aggregates by identity; cross them with events, not joins.

64
00:07:07.015 --> 00:07:07.781
Three.

65
00:07:08.821 --> 00:07:13.512
Keep the in-process domain event apart from the integration event that leaves the service.

66
00:07:14.492 --> 00:07:21.725
Next: the neighbors of DDD — orange sticky notes, dark energy and dark matter, and the team diagram that becomes the service diagram.
